<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel
    ============================
    The foundational shared library of the PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition, and the bottom of
    the dependency graph: it references nothing, and every other shared library plus all four
    in-scope services (Gateway, DataServices, Persistence, Security) reference it.

    It carries the pure behaviour ported from the legacy pfw.shared library and the pfw.common
    primitives. Both PowerBuilder targets list pfw.shared.pbl LAST in their library list, i.e. as
    the cross-target foundation resolved after everything else [pfw.pbt:LibList, pfwx.pbt:LibList]:

        RetCode.cs         the return code catalogue                (retcode.sru)
        Predicates.cs      the tri state return code algebra        (issucceeded.srf and siblings)
        Enums.cs           the constant catalogue                   (enums.sru)
        PfwException.cs    the framework exception type             (pfwexception.sru)
        ThrowException.cs  the throw helpers                        (throwexception.srf and pair)
        Formatting.cs      return code formatting and Sprintf       (formatretcode.srf, sprintf.srf)
        Bits.cs            the bit plus word and byte operations
        Ancestry.cs        the ancestry predicates
        Text.cs            the string and class name helpers

    This project holds shared IMPLEMENTATION, consumed in process through a ProjectReference. It is
    never a cross-service channel: the only published cross-service coupling in this refactor is the
    separate shared/PowerFramework.Contracts project, and this project must not become a back door
    around it. There is therefore deliberately no web, gRPC, ASP.NET, hosting, serialization, HTTP
    or database dependency here, and none may be added.

    The legacy PowerBuilder tree is the read only behavioural oracle. Nothing under ws_objects/ and
    no *.pbl, *.pbt, *.pbw, *.pbr or *.pbd is a build input of this project; the .sru files above are
    cited as the specification for the C# sources, not compiled or embedded.
  -->

  <!--
    Only genuinely local properties are set below.

    Everything else is INHERITED from the repository root Directory.Build.props through MSBuild's
    directory walk up (there is no intervening Directory.Build.props under shared/), and package
    versions come from the repository root Directory.Packages.props. TargetFramework (net10.0),
    LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, TreatWarningsAsErrors, EnableNETAnalyzers, AnalysisLevel,
    ManagePackageVersionsCentrally, Deterministic, ContinuousIntegrationBuild, Product, Company and
    Copyright are therefore ABSENT ON PURPOSE. Restating any of them here would defeat that single
    source of truth and let this project drift from the other fifteen, so do not "fix" the omission.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Both values equal the defaults MSBuild derives from this file name. They are stated
         explicitly because every consumer binds to exactly this assembly and namespace root. -->
    <RootNamespace>PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel</AssemblyName>
    <Description>Foundational shared kernel for the PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition: the return code algebra, constant catalogue, framework exception type, and the text, bit and ancestry primitives ported from the legacy pfw.shared and pfw.common libraries.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Intentionally no PackageReference: this library is pure BCL; versions, when ever needed, come
    from Directory.Packages.props and references stay versionless.

    This is a verified finding, not an oversight. Every entry in the dependency inventory was cross
    checked and none names Shared Kernel as a consumer: the ported behaviour needs only the C#
    bitwise operators, System.BitConverter, System.Type and Type.BaseType, System.String, composite
    formatting and System.Exception. Do not add a package "to be safe"; an unnecessary reference
    would also drag a NuGet audit surface onto the one project every other project depends on.
  -->

  <!--
    Intentionally no ProjectReference: Shared Kernel is the bottom of the dependency graph, so it
    references nothing and everything references it. In particular it must NOT reference
    PowerFramework.Contracts: the direction is the reverse. Contracts' common.v1.proto mirrors the
    numeric values of RetCode, but that is a value correspondence maintained by hand and documented
    in docs/CONTRACTS.md, never a compile time edge. Nor may it reference any of the four deferred
    services (DesignSystem, Documents, Integration, ScriptBridge), which are out of scope for this
    phase and have no project to reference.
  -->

  <!--
    Also deliberately absent, each for a stated reason:
      * OutputType. This is a class library and must stay one. The xunit.v3 requirement for
        OutputType Exe belongs to the sibling PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.Tests project.
      * Compile, Remove and Exclude items. The SDK globs the C# sources automatically and they all
        sit flat in this directory, so there is nothing to add and nothing to exclude.
      * InternalsVisibleTo. Every type and member here is public precisely so the sibling test
        project and the shared and service consumers reach it without one.
      * NoWarn and any project wide analyzer suppression. The legacy SCREAMING_SNAKE constant
        identifiers are preserved verbatim because they appear in serialized payloads, log records
        and characterization recordings; their naming analyzer suppressions are file glob scoped in
        the repository root .editorconfig to RetCode.cs and Enums.cs only. That scoping is
        deliberate and a project wide suppression here would broaden it.
      * GenerateDocumentationFile. Inherited TreatWarningsAsErrors turns a missing XML doc comment
        (CS1591) into a build error that would break the whole tree, and no requirement asks for a
        documentation file.
      * Any solution filter. .NET 10 emits the XML .slnx solution format, and no .slnf filter is
        authored because a filter's project list must be kept in exact agreement with the solution
        BY HAND and every drift fails hard. This line used to claim MSB4014 in all three verbs;
        that was measured false on the pinned SDK (docs/BUILD.md section 2).
  -->

</Project>
